 "facts": [
  {
   "claim": "Georges de La Tour painted The Cheat with the Ace of Diamonds in the 1630s (Louvre: c. 1635; Wikipedia: c. 1636–1638); the rich young man on the right is being fleeced by the other players; Louvre RF 1972-8",
   "source": "https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/2022-06/TRANSCRIPTION-podcast-les-enquetes-du-louvre-le-tricheur-a-l-as-de-carreau.pdf ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Card_Sharp_with_the_Ace_of_Diamonds"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Card expert Olivier Cave (Louvre podcast): the young man holds his cards with both hands, high, close to his face, 'très à découvert' (completely exposed)",
   "source": "https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/2022-06/TRANSCRIPTION-podcast-les-enquetes-du-louvre-le-tricheur-a-l-as-de-carreau.pdf"
  },
  {
   "claim": "The courtesan's sideways look; historian Élisabeth Belmas: her hand gesture may be to take the wine but rather points to the cheat, giving the order: his turn to play",
   "source": "https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/2022-06/TRANSCRIPTION-podcast-les-enquetes-du-louvre-le-tricheur-a-l-as-de-carreau.pdf"
  },
  {
   "claim": "The servant's feather on her turban and low neckline are 'not quite the usual outfit of a servant' (Belmas); her role is to divert attention from the cheat (Cave)",
   "source": "https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/2022-06/TRANSCRIPTION-podcast-les-enquetes-du-louvre-le-tricheur-a-l-as-de-carreau.pdf"
  },
  {
   "claim": "The cheat is retrieving the ace of diamonds from behind his back; his look of complicity with the viewer",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Card_Sharp_with_the_Ace_of_Diamonds ; https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/2022-06/TRANSCRIPTION-podcast-les-enquetes-du-louvre-le-tricheur-a-l-as-de-carreau.pdf"
  },
